## 3. Per-correction challenge

### 3.1 Event dirty-condition conjunction — CORRECT

Ruby, `card-1.110.0/lib/card/set/event.rb:77` — `CONDITIONS = ::Set.new(Api::OPTIONS.keys)`,
and `Api::OPTIONS` (event.rb:36-39) carries `on`, `changed`, `changing`, `when`.
`set/event/all.rb` evaluates `CONDITIONS.all? { |c| send "#{c}_condition_applies?", event, event.opts[c] }`
— **AND across condition keys**. Within one key, `changed_condition_applies?`
(all.rb:69-76) is `db_columns.any? { ... }` — **OR across columns**, with
`return true unless action == :update` and `return true if db_columns.empty?`.

Port, `src/card/mod-loader/mod-event-dispatch.ts:1127-1128`:
`if (!changedConditionApplies(event.changed, card) || !changedConditionApplies(event.changing, card))`
→ AND across keys ✓. Inside `changedConditionApplies`: `card.act?.action !== "update"` → true ✓;
`columns.length === 0` → true ✓; `columns.some(...)` → OR ✓.

Alias map: TS `DIRTY_ALIASES` `{type: "type_id", content: "db_content"}` ≡ Ruby `dirty.rb`
`dirty_aliases = { type: :type_id, content: :db_content }` ✓ (Ruby additionally aliases the
`*_is_changing?` method names, so the raw declared name works there; the TS normalises
to the canonical column name instead — equivalent, since the engine's `changedFields`
carry the canonical names).

**Invocation path resolved:** `runModEventStage` → `dispatchModEvent` → the gate at
:1127 precedes method resolution and body invocation. The gate is evaluated per event
declaration, so an event declaring both keys must satisfy both.

The prior reviewer's finding (`adversarial-review-2026-10-27-codex`, "a second dirty
condition is discarded by the event gate") is **corrected**, and the committed control
covers both orders (only-`changed`, only-`changing`, both, neither) plus the empty-list
cases that the first repair got wrong (`changing: []` must not suppress a non-empty
`changed:`). Ruby-reference check ✓; source control ✓; independent rerun ✓.

### 3.2 Overlapping set precedence — CORRECT

The prior finding was "overlapping set overrides are rejected with a missing-precedence
error". The port now separates callback scheduling from method resolution:
`mod-event-dispatch.ts` resolves the winning definition by normalized full module
identity (no mod name), and raises a **named** error only when the order genuinely
cannot be established — `requires the loaded module order to resolve duplicate
definitions` / `requires explicit card module ancestry after a name/type change`
(:1014, :920). It never guesses registration order and never substitutes the
renderer's format ancestry for card/event ancestry.

Ruby basis: `set/registrar.rb:74-76` (first Module registration position retained),
`set/pattern/all.rb:44-46` and `mod/core/set/all/initialize.rb:61-73` (inclusion
order), `set/event.rb:97-100,133-155` (callback definition vs method definition).

The 35-case control covers cross-module duplicates, cross-pattern precedence, numeric
path normalisation, callback-chain resets and separate stage occurrences. Ruby-reference
check ✓; source control ✓; independent rerun ✓. The named-error boundary is the doc's
declared "explicit remaining ancestry boundary"
(`adversarial-mod-event-repairs-2026-10-28.md:125-140`), not a hidden gap.

### 3.3 Type-change save validation — CORRECT

Prior finding: "new-type event validation reads the old type (full-save confirmation pending)".

Invocation path resolved: the engine's validate snapshot carries the **assigned** type
(`card-action-engine.ts:4478` `typeId: incomingTypeId`) while keeping the pre-act type
separately (`typeIdBeforeAct: row.type_id`, `patternTypeId: row.type_id`, :4482-4483).
Membership for an ordinary (non-`changing:`) event therefore resolves against the NEW
type: `conditionCard` (:726-730) keeps `card.typeId` when the event declares no
`changing:`, and `setConditionApplies` (:765) applies it before the type-membership
lookup. For a `changing:` event the original receiver's attached roots are used instead
(:751-764), matching Ruby's `set_condition_card(old_sets)` branch at
`set/event/all.rb:42-47` and the executed-Ruby correction recorded at
`adversarial-mod-event-repairs-2026-10-28.md:26-30` (`refresh_set_modules` retains old
singleton modules and includes newly assigned modules too).

The committed control is a **full save**, not a unit probe: a Basic card containing
nonnumeric text is updated to Number and the registered Number validator must reject
it, with the entire enclosing act rolled back — under both fallback and serving
membership — plus a numeric control that succeeds, a nested type-changing save, and
old/new/renamed attachment-root coverage. Ruby-reference check ✓; source control ✓;
independent rerun ✓. This closes the prior reviewer's explicitly pending item.

